-- 024 — Add 55 language questions
--
-- Pure insert. Ids are deterministic: md5(section || '|' || prompt)::UUID,
-- so re-running this file is a no-op. Nothing existing is deleted or updated.

WITH q(prompt, options, correct_index, difficulty, explanation) AS (VALUES
  ('Which sentence contains an error?', '["The boxes in the attic is covered with dust.", "My brother and I walked to the library.", "Each of the students has a locker.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 0, 1, 'Subject-verb agreement: the subject is the plural noun ''boxes'' (not ''attic''), so the verb must be ''are covered.'' A prepositional phrase between the subject and verb never changes the number of the verb.'),
  ('Which sentence contains a mistake?', '["We stayed inside because it was raining.", "The movie was long, we enjoyed it anyway.", "After dinner, we washed the dishes.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 1, 1, 'This is a comma splice: two independent clauses (''The movie was long'' and ''we enjoyed it anyway'') joined by only a comma. Fix it with a semicolon or with a comma plus ''but.'''),
  ('Choose the sentence that contains an error.', '["Our class visited the Statue of Liberty in July.", "We drove north on saturday morning.", "Mrs. Alvarez teaches Spanish at our school.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 1, 1, 'Days of the week are proper nouns and must be capitalized: ''Saturday.'' (The direction ''north'' is correctly lowercase here because it names a direction, not a region.)'),
  ('Identify the sentence that contains an error.', '["The dog wagged it''s tail.", "The girls'' team won the championship.", "That is Marcus''s bicycle.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 0, 1, '''It''s'' is the contraction for ''it is.'' The possessive form of ''it'' is ''its,'' with no apostrophe: ''The dog wagged its tail.'''),
  ('Select the sentence that contains an error.', '["My cousin, who lives in Denver, is visiting us next week.", "We packed sandwiches, apples, and water for the hike.", "Dr. Reyes asked whether I had finished the assignment.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 3, 1, 'All three sentences are correct: the first sets off a nonessential clause with a pair of commas, the second uses commas in a series, and the third uses ''whether'' correctly in an indirect question. The answer is ''No mistakes.'''),
  ('Find the sentence that contains an error.', '["The coach gave Elena and I extra practice time.", "She and I studied together for the test.", "Between you and me, the test was easy.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 0, 1, 'Pronoun case: ''Elena and I'' is the indirect object of ''gave,'' so the object pronoun is needed: ''gave Elena and me.'' Test it by dropping the other name — ''gave I extra time'' is clearly wrong.'),
  ('Which of the following sentences contains an error?', '["I didn''t see nobody in the hallway.", "There wasn''t anything left in the refrigerator.", "We hardly ever go downtown.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 0, 1, 'Double negative: ''didn''t'' and ''nobody'' cancel each other out. Standard English uses one negative — ''I didn''t see anybody in the hallway.'''),
  ('Which of these sentences contains a mistake?', '["The bell rang the students hurried to class.", "The bell rang, and the students hurried to class.", "When the bell rang, the students hurried to class.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 0, 1, 'This is a run-on (fused) sentence: two independent clauses are jammed together with no punctuation or conjunction. Add a comma plus ''and,'' or a semicolon.'),
  ('One of these sentences contains an error. Which one?', '["We read a poem by Langston Hughes in English class.", "My family drives to Lake Tahoe every winter.", "I asked professor Kim about the homework.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 2, 1, 'A title used directly before a person''s name is part of the name and is capitalized: ''Professor Kim.'' (''English'' is capitalized in the first sentence because it comes from a proper noun.)'),
  ('Which sentence below contains an error?', '["Yesterday she brought her lunch from home.", "He has went to the dentist twice this year.", "They were sitting on the porch when we arrived.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 1, 1, 'Wrong verb form: ''went'' is the simple past of ''go,'' but a helping verb like ''has'' requires the past participle: ''He has gone to the dentist.'''),
  ('Which sentence is written incorrectly?', '["Where did you put my calculator?", "What an incredible finish that was!", "Do you know what time the bus leaves.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 2, 1, 'End punctuation: ''Do you know what time the bus leaves'' is a direct question and must end with a question mark, not a period.'),
  ('Which sentence needs to be corrected?', '["The team practiced all week, however, it lost the game.", "The team practiced all week; nevertheless, it lost the game.", "Although the team practiced all week, it lost the game.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 0, 2, '''However'' is a conjunctive adverb, not a coordinating conjunction, so it cannot join two independent clauses with commas alone. This is a comma splice; use a semicolon before ''however'' and a comma after it.'),
  ('Which sentence contains a writing error?', '["Running down the street, my backpack slipped off my shoulder.", "As I ran down the street, my backpack slipped off my shoulder.", "My backpack slipped off my shoulder while I was running.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 0, 2, 'Dangling modifier: the introductory phrase ''Running down the street'' has to describe the subject that follows, and here that subject is ''my backpack'' — which was not running.'),
  ('Which sentence contains a mechanical error?', '["Neither the coach nor the players were satisfied with the result.", "The principal, along with two teachers, is attending the conference.", "Everyone brought his or her own lunch.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 3, 2, 'All three are correct. With ''neither/nor'' the verb agrees with the nearer subject (''players'' — were); a phrase like ''along with two teachers'' does not change the singular subject ''principal''; and ''everyone'' is singular, matching ''his or her.'' The answer is ''No mistakes.'''),
  ('Which sentence has an error in it?', '["Each of the boys brought his own glove.", "Every store on the block must display their prices in the window.", "The team celebrated its victory.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 1, 2, 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement: ''Every store'' is singular, so the pronoun must be singular — ''must display its prices.'''),
  ('Which sentence contains an error in punctuation, capitalization, or usage?', '["I have one goal this year; to make the honor roll.", "We left early; the traffic was terrible.", "Bring the following items: a pen, a notebook, and a calculator.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 0, 2, 'A semicolon must join two independent clauses. ''To make the honor roll'' is not a clause, so a colon (or a dash) is needed instead.'),
  ('Which sentence is NOT written correctly?', '["The book, that I borrowed from Jenna, is overdue.", "The book that I borrowed from Jenna is overdue.", "My oldest sister, who lives in Chicago, is a nurse.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 0, 2, 'A ''that'' clause is essential — it identifies which book — so it must not be set off by commas. Only nonessential clauses (usually introduced by ''who'' or ''which'') take commas.'),
  ('Point out the sentence that contains an error.', '["Marcus likes hiking, swimming, and to ride his bike.", "Marcus likes hiking, swimming, and biking.", "Marcus likes to hike, to swim, and to bike.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 0, 2, 'Faulty parallelism: items in a series must share the same grammatical form. Two ''-ing'' forms followed by an infinitive (''to ride'') breaks the pattern.'),
  ('Which one of these sentences is incorrect?', '["The Wilsons are hosting the party.", "Both of my sister''s are in college.", "The children''s coats are in the closet.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 1, 2, 'Apostrophe misuse: ''sisters'' here is a simple plural, not a possessive, so it needs no apostrophe — ''Both of my sisters are in college.'''),
  ('Which sentence should be revised because it contains an error?', '["They''re going to meet us at the theater.", "The runners left there water bottles on the bench.", "There are three reasons I disagree.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 1, 2, 'Wrong word: ''there'' indicates place. The possessive pronoun ''their'' is needed — ''left their water bottles.'''),
  ('Which sentence in this group contains an error?', '["We moved to the South when I was six.", "My favorite season is Autumn.", "Turn west at the second traffic light.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 1, 2, 'Seasons are common nouns and are not capitalized: ''autumn.'' (''the South'' is capitalized because it names a region, and ''west'' is lowercase because it names a direction.)'),
  ('Which sentence contains an error in standard written English?', '["\"I''ll be there at six,\" said Dana.", "Mr. Ruiz said, \"Please open your books\".", "Have you read \"The Necklace\"?", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 1, 2, 'In American usage, a period always goes inside the closing quotation marks: said, "Please open your books." (A question mark goes outside only when the quoted material itself is not the question, as in the third sentence.)'),
  ('Read the sentences. Which one contains an error?', '["Because the road was closed, we took a detour through town.", "The recipe calls for flour, sugar, and two eggs.", "Neither answer seems correct to me.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 3, 2, 'All three are correct: an introductory dependent clause is followed by a comma, the series is punctuated correctly, and ''neither'' is singular and takes the singular verb ''seems.'' The answer is ''No mistakes.'''),
  ('Which sentence contains an error that must be fixed?', '["I laid the book on the table last night.", "The cat likes to lay in the sunshine.", "She has lain down for a nap.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 1, 2, '''Lay'' takes an object (you lay something down); ''lie'' means to recline. The cat likes to lie in the sunshine.'),
  ('Which of the sentences below is incorrect?', '["Of the two routes, the shorter one goes past the park.", "This is the most unusual painting in the gallery.", "Of my three cousins, Rosa is the oldest.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 3, 2, 'All three are correct. Use the comparative (''shorter'') for two things and the superlative (''oldest,'' ''most unusual'') for three or more — each sentence follows the rule, so the answer is ''No mistakes.'''),
  ('Which sentence contains a grammar or punctuation error?', '["Because the storm knocked out the power.", "The storm knocked out the power for six hours.", "When the power returned, we cheered.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 0, 2, 'Sentence fragment: ''Because the storm knocked out the power'' is a dependent clause with no main clause to complete it.'),
  ('Which sentence is incorrect as written?', '["The number of applicants has increased.", "A number of applicants have withdrawn.", "Statistics is my brother''s favorite subject.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 3, 3, 'All three are correct. ''The number'' is singular (''has''), ''a number'' means ''several'' and is plural (''have''), and a subject like ''statistics'' naming a field of study takes a singular verb. The answer is ''No mistakes.'''),
  ('Which sentence, as written, contains an error?', '["The award went to whoever finished first.", "Give the message to whomever answers the phone.", "I know whom they selected.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 1, 3, '''Whoever'' is the subject of the verb ''answers,'' so the subject form is required: ''to whoever answers the phone.'' The whole clause, not the single word, is the object of ''to.'''),
  ('Which sentence in the group is incorrect?', '["After finishing the exam, the answer sheets were collected by the proctor.", "After the students finished the exam, the proctor collected the answer sheets.", "Having finished the exam, the students handed in their answer sheets.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 0, 3, 'Dangling modifier: the introductory phrase ''After finishing the exam'' must describe the sentence''s subject, but the subject is ''the answer sheets,'' which cannot finish an exam.'),
  ('Which sentence contains an error in its wording or punctuation?', '["The climate of Arizona is drier than Oregon.", "The climate of Arizona is drier than that of Oregon.", "Arizona is drier than Oregon.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 0, 3, 'Illogical comparison: the sentence compares a climate to a state. Add ''that of'' so climate is compared with climate.'),
  ('Which sentence contains an error a careful proofreader would catch?', '["We visited Rome, Italy; Lyon, France; and Bern, Switzerland.", "The panel included Dr. Lee, a chemist; Ms. Ortiz, an engineer; and Mr. Hall, a teacher.", "Our stops were Austin, Texas, Tulsa, Oklahoma, and Topeka, Kansas.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 2, 3, 'When the items in a series already contain commas, the items must be separated by semicolons: ''Austin, Texas; Tulsa, Oklahoma; and Topeka, Kansas.'' Otherwise the reader cannot tell where one item ends.'),
  ('Which sentence fails to follow standard writing rules?', '["If I was you, I would apologize.", "If I were taller, I would play center.", "She asked that he be on time.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 0, 3, 'Subjunctive mood: for a condition contrary to fact, use ''were,'' not ''was'' — ''If I were you.'''),
  ('Which sentence contains an error that should be corrected?', '["She gave a well-written speech.", "The speech was well written.", "We hired a part-time coach for the season.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 3, 3, 'All three are correct. A compound modifier is hyphenated before the noun (''well-written speech,'' ''part-time coach'') but not after the verb (''was well written''). The answer is ''No mistakes.'''),
  ('Which sentence would an editor mark as incorrect?', '["When Jordan met Alex, he was carrying a heavy backpack.", "Jordan was carrying a heavy backpack when he met Alex.", "When Jordan met Alex, Alex was carrying a heavy backpack.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 0, 3, 'Ambiguous pronoun reference: ''he'' could refer to Jordan or to Alex, so the reader cannot tell who had the backpack. A pronoun must have one clear antecedent.'),
  ('Look closely at each sentence. Which one contains an error?', '["Not only did the storm flood the streets, but it also toppled several trees.", "The committee has reached its decision, and it will announce the results tomorrow.", "Having studied all weekend, Marisol felt confident about the exam.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 3, 3, 'All three are correct: ''not only ... but also'' is properly paired, the collective noun ''committee'' takes the singular ''has'' and ''its,'' and the introductory participial phrase correctly describes the subject ''Marisol.'' The answer is ''No mistakes.'''),
  ('Which sentence contains an error in the use of punctuation?', '["The ingredients we need are: eggs, milk, and butter.", "We need three ingredients: eggs, milk, and butter.", "Her reasoning was simple: practice makes perfect.", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 0, 3, 'A colon must follow a complete independent clause. Here it separates the verb ''are'' from its complement, so no colon should be used at all.'),
  ('Which word is spelled incorrectly?', '["definately", "separate", "calendar", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 0, 1, 'The correct spelling is ''definitely'' — it comes from ''finite,'' so there is no ''a'' in the middle.'),
  ('Which word is misspelled?', '["believe", "recieve", "weight", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 1, 1, 'The correct spelling is ''receive.'' The rule is ''i before e except after c,'' and ''receive'' follows c.'),
  ('Choose the word that is spelled incorrectly.', '["friend", "because", "tomorow", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 2, 1, 'The correct spelling is ''tomorrow,'' with one ''m'' and two ''r''s.'),
  ('Identify the misspelled word.', '["necessary", "occurrence", "embarrassment", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 3, 2, 'All three are spelled correctly: ''necessary'' (one c, two s''s), ''occurrence'' (two c''s, two r''s), and ''embarrassment'' (two r''s, two s''s). The answer is ''No mistakes.'''),
  ('Which of these words is misspelled?', '["priviledge", "conscience", "rhythm", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 0, 2, 'The correct spelling is ''privilege'' — there is no ''d'' in the word.'),
  ('Select the word that is misspelled.', '["maintenance", "perseverance", "independant", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 2, 2, 'The correct spelling is ''independent,'' ending in ''-ent'' rather than ''-ant.'''),
  ('Which word below is spelled incorrectly?', '["accommodate", "conscientious", "liaison", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 3, 3, 'All three are spelled correctly: ''accommodate'' (two c''s, two m''s), ''conscientious,'' and ''liaison'' (i-a-i). The answer is ''No mistakes.'''),
  ('Find the misspelled word.', '["questionnaire", "harrass", "millennium", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 1, 3, 'The correct spelling is ''harass'' — one r and two s''s.'),
  ('One of these words is misspelled. Which one?', '["acquaintance", "occassionally", "bureaucracy", "No mistakes"]'::JSONB, 1, 3, 'The correct spelling is ''occasionally'' — two c''s but only one s.'),
  ('Choose the best way to join these two ideas: ''Ella practiced every day.'' and ''She still missed the final shot.''', '["Ella practiced every day, but she still missed the final shot.", "Ella practiced every day, so she still missed the final shot.", "Ella practiced every day, for she still missed the final shot.", "Ella practiced every day, and therefore she still missed the final shot."]'::JSONB, 0, 1, 'The two ideas contrast: hard practice did not prevent the miss. ''But'' signals contrast, while ''so,'' ''for,'' and ''therefore'' all signal cause or result, which reverses the meaning.'),
  ('Which sentence is the best topic sentence for a paragraph about why students should get enough sleep?', '["Sleep is something everyone does.", "Getting eight to ten hours of sleep helps students think clearly, stay healthy, and do better in school.", "I usually go to bed around ten o''clock.", "Scientists have studied many things about the human body."]'::JSONB, 1, 1, 'A topic sentence states the paragraph''s main idea and previews its support. Only this option names the subject (student sleep) and the point the paragraph will prove; the others are too vague or are single details.'),
  ('Which sentence does not belong in this paragraph? ''Recycling saves natural resources. Making new aluminum cans from recycled metal uses far less energy than making them from ore. Recycling paper spares thousands of trees each year. My uncle drives a red pickup truck.''', '["Recycling saves natural resources.", "Making new aluminum cans from recycled metal uses far less energy than making them from ore.", "Recycling paper spares thousands of trees each year.", "My uncle drives a red pickup truck."]'::JSONB, 3, 1, 'Every sentence in a paragraph must support the main idea. The main idea is that recycling saves resources; a detail about an uncle''s truck is unrelated.'),
  ('Which sentence expresses the idea most clearly?', '["Being that the weather was bad, the game was canceled by the officials.", "Due to the fact that the weather was bad, a cancellation of the game was made.", "Because of bad weather, the officials canceled the game.", "The game, on account of bad weather, was given a cancellation by officials."]'::JSONB, 2, 2, 'The clearest version is direct and active: a named subject (''the officials'') performs the verb (''canceled''). The others use wordy phrases (''being that,'' ''due to the fact that,'' ''on account of'') and awkward passive constructions.'),
  ('Which word best completes the sentence? ''The trail was steep and rocky; ______, we reached the summit before noon.''', '["therefore", "nevertheless", "similarly", "for example"]'::JSONB, 1, 2, 'The second clause states an unexpected result despite the difficulty, so a contrast transition is needed. ''Nevertheless'' signals contrast; ''therefore'' signals result, ''similarly'' signals comparison, and ''for example'' introduces an illustration.'),
  ('Read the paragraph. Which sentence should be removed? ''Bees pollinate a third of the food we eat. Without them, many crops would fail. Beekeepers report that colonies have been shrinking for years. Honey tastes delicious on warm biscuits.''', '["Bees pollinate a third of the food we eat.", "Without them, many crops would fail.", "Beekeepers report that colonies have been shrinking for years.", "Honey tastes delicious on warm biscuits."]'::JSONB, 3, 2, 'The paragraph is about the importance of bees to the food supply and the threat to their colonies. A remark about how honey tastes does not support that idea and breaks the paragraph''s unity.'),
  ('Which sentence would best conclude a paragraph arguing that schools should start later in the morning?', '["Some schools start at 7:30 a.m.", "Teenagers naturally fall asleep later at night.", "A later start time would give students the rest they need to learn well.", "Sleep is important for everyone, including adults."]'::JSONB, 2, 2, 'A concluding sentence restates the argument''s main point. This option ties the evidence back to the claim; the others add a new fact, repeat a supporting detail, or drift off the topic.'),
  ('Which sentence best combines these two sentences? ''The museum opened in 1908.'' and ''It houses the largest collection of fossils in the state.''', '["The museum opened in 1908, it houses the largest collection of fossils in the state.", "The museum, which opened in 1908, houses the largest collection of fossils in the state.", "The museum opened in 1908 because it houses the largest collection of fossils in the state.", "Opening in 1908, the largest collection of fossils in the state is housed by the museum."]'::JSONB, 1, 3, 'Subordinating the less important fact in a ''which'' clause keeps the main idea in the main clause. The first option is a comma splice, the third invents a false cause-and-effect, and the fourth dangles the phrase ''Opening in 1908'' onto the wrong subject.'),
  ('Where does this sentence best fit? ''That changed when the city built a footbridge over the creek.'' Paragraph: (1) For years the park was hard to reach. (2) Families on the north side had to drive nearly two miles around. (3) Now children walk there in ten minutes. (4) Attendance has tripled.', '["Before sentence 1", "Between sentences 1 and 2", "Between sentences 2 and 3", "After sentence 4"]'::JSONB, 2, 3, 'The word ''That'' must point back to the problem described in sentences 1 and 2, and the sentence must come before ''Now,'' which describes the result. Only the position between sentences 2 and 3 satisfies both.'),
  ('Which revision states the idea most effectively?', '["The reason why the experiment failed was because of the fact that the sample was contaminated.", "The experiment failed because the sample was contaminated.", "The experiment''s failure was caused by contamination that was present in the sample.", "Contamination of the sample is what caused the experiment to have failed."]'::JSONB, 1, 3, 'The best revision says the same thing with no wasted words and a clear subject-verb-cause structure. ''The reason why ... was because'' is redundant, and the other two options bury the action in nouns and awkward verb phrases.')
)
INSERT INTO questions (id, section, passage, prompt, options, correct_index, difficulty, explanation)
SELECT md5('language' || '|' || q.prompt)::UUID, 'language'::section_type, NULL,
       q.prompt, q.options, q.correct_index, q.difficulty, q.explanation
FROM q
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ── Verification: expected 55 rows ──────────────────────────────────
SELECT '024 language' AS migration, COUNT(*) AS present, 55 AS expected
FROM questions
WHERE id IN (
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence contains an error?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence contains a mistake?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Choose the sentence that contains an error.')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Identify the sentence that contains an error.')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Select the sentence that contains an error.')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Find the sentence that contains an error.')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which of the following sentences contains an error?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which of these sentences contains a mistake?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'One of these sentences contains an error. Which one?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence below contains an error?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence is written incorrectly?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence needs to be corrected?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence contains a writing error?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence contains a mechanical error?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence has an error in it?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence contains an error in punctuation, capitalization, or usage?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence is NOT written correctly?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Point out the sentence that contains an error.')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which one of these sentences is incorrect?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence should be revised because it contains an error?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence in this group contains an error?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence contains an error in standard written English?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Read the sentences. Which one contains an error?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence contains an error that must be fixed?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which of the sentences below is incorrect?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence contains a grammar or punctuation error?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence is incorrect as written?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence, as written, contains an error?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence in the group is incorrect?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence contains an error in its wording or punctuation?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence contains an error a careful proofreader would catch?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence fails to follow standard writing rules?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence contains an error that should be corrected?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence would an editor mark as incorrect?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Look closely at each sentence. Which one contains an error?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence contains an error in the use of punctuation?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which word is spelled incorrectly?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which word is misspelled?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Choose the word that is spelled incorrectly.')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Identify the misspelled word.')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which of these words is misspelled?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Select the word that is misspelled.')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which word below is spelled incorrectly?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Find the misspelled word.')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'One of these words is misspelled. Which one?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Choose the best way to join these two ideas: ''Ella practiced every day.'' and ''She still missed the final shot.''')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence is the best topic sentence for a paragraph about why students should get enough sleep?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence does not belong in this paragraph? ''Recycling saves natural resources. Making new aluminum cans from recycled metal uses far less energy than making them from ore. Recycling paper spares thousands of trees each year. My uncle drives a red pickup truck.''')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence expresses the idea most clearly?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which word best completes the sentence? ''The trail was steep and rocky; ______, we reached the summit before noon.''')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Read the paragraph. Which sentence should be removed? ''Bees pollinate a third of the food we eat. Without them, many crops would fail. Beekeepers report that colonies have been shrinking for years. Honey tastes delicious on warm biscuits.''')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence would best conclude a paragraph arguing that schools should start later in the morning?')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which sentence best combines these two sentences? ''The museum opened in 1908.'' and ''It houses the largest collection of fossils in the state.''')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Where does this sentence best fit? ''That changed when the city built a footbridge over the creek.'' Paragraph: (1) For years the park was hard to reach. (2) Families on the north side had to drive nearly two miles around. (3) Now children walk there in ten minutes. (4) Attendance has tripled.')::UUID,
  md5('language' || '|' || 'Which revision states the idea most effectively?')::UUID
);
